import * from net;

mod export mysql;

// DOCS -- public `mysql` module (UNIFIED_CORE_LIBRARY.md §4). This is the
// portable front of the MySQL wire client. It is a PROTOCOL module (§4
// rule 7): it contains ZERO OS code -- no win32 imports, no `#on` regions,
// no os module gates -- and every byte on the wire flows through the
// universal socket core std/net.sr (connectRaw/recvRaw/closeRaw/
// setRecvTimeoutRaw), whose Windows regions hold the ws2_32 calls. The one
// piece of wire framing here is the initial handshake greeting parse --
// the private readGreeting() over net primitives (see that function). This
// module is pure MySQL protocol logic and would compile unchanged on any
// backend that provides net.
//
// FAILURE STYLE: connect() returns a connection with no greeting
// accepted, query()/insert() stay silent no-ops (query's empty result,
// insert's 0), close() stays a no-op, getLastReply() stays "".
//
// DISPATCH (per compiler, probed): `mysql` is builtin-reserved on NEITHER
// compiler -- silverc-rs's builtin_modules_set (src/main.rs:81 lists
// fs/json/yaml/env/url/hash/path/bom/console/math/http only) and the
// self-hosted compiler's visBuiltinModule (compiler/visibility.sr:605 lists
// fs/json/yaml/llmai/LLMAI/os/project/exception/bom/process/notifications/
// env/url/hash/path/console/math/http) plus its driver prepend list all
// exclude it (grep for "mysql" under silverc-rs/src/ and silverc/compiler/
// finds nothing; the only hit anywhere is mysql.sr's own entry in
// silverc.srp's exclude list, which only keeps this module out of the
// compiler's own aggregate build -- the same posture as sql.sr/ssh.sr/
// smtp.sr/pop.sr/imap.sr; it does not make `mysql` a builtin). Both
// compilers resolve `import * from mysql;` as an ORDINARY SOURCE module
// (std/mysql.sr); no native dispatch exists.
//
// HONESTY NOTE -- this is NOT an authenticated MySQL client: real MySQL
// needs the full handshake (the server picks the auth plugin;
// mysql_native_password needs SHA1 -- SHA1(password) XOR
// SHA1(scramble + SHA1(SHA1(password))); caching_sha2_password needs TLS or
// RSA), and NONE of that is implemented here. std/hash on both compilers
// is djb2/fnv1 only (silverc-rs src/stdlib/hash.rs gen_hash_call and the
// self-host std/hash.sr expose no md5/sha at all), so the
// mysql_native_password scramble CANNOT be built -- auth is documented
// UNSUPPORTED, not faked. No HandshakeResponse41 is ever sent (sending one
// with an unscrambleable garbage auth response would be pretending), so
// there is NO session: connect() performs the one MySQL wire exchange that
// precedes any authentication -- it opens a real TCP connection (dotted-IP
// host, MySQL's default port 3306; the connect signature has no port
// parameter), reads and parses the server's initial handshake greeting
// packet (protocol version byte, NUL-terminated server version string --
// see the NUL LANDMINE for why the parse happens on int[] bytes), surfaces
// what it saw through the serverVersion()/protocolVersion()/getLastReply()
// getters, and closes the socket. isConnected() stays false -- the honest
// terminal state, since authentication never happens. Against a non-MySQL
// listener the greeting parse fails earlier and equally gracefully: the
// connection comes back with no greeting accepted and the process exits
// cleanly. No exceptions anywhere. The user/pass/db parameters are carried
// unused by the exchange (stored for a future session implementation).
//
// query()/insert() NEVER touch the network on this build: there is no
// authenticated session to run a statement on, and bytes sent to a MySQL
// server before the handshake completes are misread as handshake data.
// query() returns an EMPTY result (rows stay empty -- no result-set
// framing exists to interpret anything with); insert() returns 0 -- the
// affected-rows count is not available over this wire protocol, and 0 is
// the honest count for a statement that never executed.
//
// WIRE BEHAVIOR: the only working exchange is connect()'s greeting read
// -- one real TCP connection through net.connectRaw with the 30 s
// SO_RCVTIMEO armed by net.setRecvTimeoutRaw; serverVersion()/
// protocolVersion()/getLastReply() keep what the exchange observed; insert()
// returns 0 (nothing executed) and both query overloads return an empty
// result via a differently-named private implementation.
//
// SCOPE: no authentication of any kind (see the honesty note), no
// HandshakeResponse41, no OK/ERR packet handling, no command phase, no
// COM_QUERY framing, no result-set framing, no prepared statements, no
// compression, no TLS. The greeting's payload length, sequence id,
// scramble and capability flags are read past but not validated (the
// lenient ssh bannerOk posture: the protocol byte must be 10 and the
// version string must be NUL-terminated -- nothing else is checked; the
// NUL LANDMINE constrains how the terminator is observed through
// net.recvRaw's string, see readGreeting).
//
// NUL LANDMINE (on both compilers; contract unchanged in
// silverc/compiler/mini_cg.sr's genStringConcat): an embedded NUL byte is
// silently DROPPED when concatenated into a string (a probe built
// char(19),char(0),char(0),char(0),char(10),"8.0.32",char(0),char(200) --
// the resulting string is 9 bytes: every char(0) vanishes, byte-identically
// on silverc-rs and the self-host). The MySQL greeting is full of NULs
// (header high bytes, sequence id, the version terminator), so the raw
// packet can never ride the string concat path. The parse therefore works
// on packed int[] buffers (NUL values survive in an int[] slot) and returns
// only a NUL-free summary string. That contract holds over the net
// primitives: net.recvRaw delivers the chunk as a string, so
// the header bytes and any embedded NULs are invisible to the parse (see
// readGreeting for the byte-exact shape this implies and why the visible
// bytes pin the same summary the int[] parse produced).
//
// OVERLOAD DISPATCH BUG (compiler-level, same shape std/sql.sr documents):
// same-name METHOD overloads with DIFFERENT ARITIES dispatch by NAME to the
// LAST-DECLARED overload, ignoring arity, and a same-name delegation inside
// an overload body (`this.query(sql)` inside the two-arg query)
// re-dispatches to ITSELF and recurses forever (exit 0xC00000FD, stack
// overflow, on both compilers). KNOWN LIMITS (no compiler change): the two
// query overloads do NOT delegate to this.query -- each calls the
// differently-named private runQuery and carries its params unused, so both
// overloads are behaviorally identical and the name-only dispatch lands
// safely whichever overload it picks. insert() is single-declaration (no
// overloads), so it is unaffected.
//
// PUBLIC API: class row -- construct(), get(string) -> string; class
// result -- construct(); class connection -- construct(string host, string
// user, string pass, string db), query(string) -> result, query(string,
// object[]) -> result (both overloads), insert(string, object[])
// -> int, close(); free function connect(string, string, string, string)
// -> connection. Every name (row, result, connection, get, query, insert,
// close, connect, host, user, pass, db, has, hasNext, next, runQuery,
// absorb, defaultPort, greetingOk, greetingProtocol, greetingVersion,
// getLastReply, isConnected, serverVersion, protocolVersion, lastReply,
// protocolVer, serverVer, names, values, rows, idx, info) is keyword-free
// on both compilers -- checked against silverc-rs src/token.rs and the
// self-host compiler/lexer.sr keyword tables (grep: zero hits). The
// dangerous SQL-adjacent identifiers are clear: `from`/`to`/`match` are
// keywords on BOTH compilers and appear nowhere in this API; `insert` is
// a keyword on NEITHER (it is a method name here). `object[]` params
// are used as-is (the type parses on both compilers -- the std/sql.sr
// probe).
//
// ADDITIONAL SURFACE (the std/ssh.sr / std/sql.sr precedent):
// connection.getLastReply() returns the greeting summary string (protocol
// byte + server version chars) after a successful
// greeting, "" before the first connect, after a failed connect, or when
// the peer sent no parseable greeting (mysql reads are packet-framed, so
// unlike ssh's text banner there is no useful text surface for junk -- the
// graceful signal is isConnected() staying false and protocolVersion()
// staying 0); connection.isConnected() is true only after a COMPLETED
// authenticated session, which never happens on this build (authentication
// is unimplemented -- see the honesty note); connection.serverVersion()
// returns the greeting's server version string (e.g. "8.0.32"; "" when no
// greeting is accepted); connection.protocolVersion() returns the
// greeting's protocol version byte (10 on real servers; 0 when none is
// accepted); row.has(name) is the containment test (get returns null for
// absent names, so has() is the non-null check callers want);
// result.hasNext()/next() give the (currently always empty) rows an
// iteration surface so a future protocol can fill them without an API
// change -- hasNext() is false and next() null on this build; free pure
// helpers -- defaultPort() (3306, MySQL's default port; the connect
// signature carries no port parameter), greetingOk(info) (true
// when `info` carries a protocol-10 byte and a non-empty version string),
// greetingProtocol(info) (the protocol byte, 0 when absent) and
// greetingVersion(info) (the version tail after the protocol byte, "" when
// absent) are pure and probeable byte-identically on both compilers. Also
// invisible internals: the private lastReply/protocolVer/serverVer
// fields (initialized in construct), the private absorb/runQuery plumbing,
// row's two parallel `= []`-initialized string[] arrays (the KVP LANDMINE
// std/sql.sr probed: an uninitialized kvp silently swallows writes on rs
// and access-violates on the self-host), and result's `= []`-initialized
// row[] field.
//
// CALLER NOTES: instantiate with `var c = connect("127.0.0.1", "user",
// "pass", "db");` -- a lowercase class arriving from a star-imported
// module cannot be spelled as a local's type on silverc-rs (per-file
// parsing; `var` works on both, probed via std/ftps.sr) and `new
// mod.connection()` dies on BOTH compilers. NOTE the name collision:
// std/sql.sr (a DIFFERENT module) also exports a class
// `connection` and a free `connect` -- a file that star-imports BOTH
// modules collides; pick one per file (or alias). The host
// must be a dotted IP (inet_addr posture -- no name resolution, same as
// std/ssh.sr's client); the port is always defaultPort() (3306). Strings
// returned across this module's boundary (getLastReply/serverVersion and
// the free helpers) should be assigned to a local before being
// concatenated into larger expressions (silverc-rs stringifies the return
// pointer in expression position; see std/dns.sr's RS-ONLY CALLER
// LANDMINE). After connect(), check protocolVersion() != 0 (or
// greetingOk(c.getLastReply()) with the reply in a local) to know whether
// a real MySQL server greeted; isConnected() is always false on this
// build. query() always returns an exhausted result and insert() always
// returns 0 -- no session exists (see the honesty note). close() is a
// no-op: connect() closes its own socket before
// returning, so there is no session handle left to close.

// MySQL's default port -- the port every connect() uses (the connect
// signature carries no port parameter). Pure; probeable.
public int defaultPort() {
    return 3306;
}

// True when `info` is a parsed greeting summary: a protocol-10 byte
// followed by at least one version character. Pure; probeable.
public bool greetingOk(string info) {
    if (info.length < 2) { return false; }
    return int(info[0]) == 10;
}

// The protocol version byte of a greeting summary (10 on real servers);
// 0 when the summary is empty. Pure; probeable.
public int greetingProtocol(string info) {
    if (info.length < 1) { return 0; }
    return int(info[0]);
}

// The server version tail of a greeting summary (everything after the
// protocol byte, e.g. "8.0.32"); "" when absent. Pure; probeable.
public string greetingVersion(string info) {
    if (info.length < 2) { return ""; }
    return sliceRange(info, 1, info.length);
}

class row {
    private string[] names;
    private string[] values;

    construct {
        this.names = [];
        this.values = [];
    }

    // Returns the value stored under `name`, or null when the name is
    // absent (see the KVP LANDMINE in the docs for why the storage is two
    // parallel `= []`-initialized arrays: an uninitialized kvp silently
    // returns null on rs and access-violates once it is written on the
    // self-host). On this build rows are never populated
    // (no result-set framing exists -- see the honesty note), so every
    // get() returns null; the arrays exist so a future protocol can fill
    // rows without an API change.
    public string get(string name) {
        for (int i = 0; i < this.names.length; i++) {
            if (this.names[i] == name) {
                return this.values[i];
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    // True when `name` is present (get returns null for absent
    // names, so has() is the non-null check callers want).
    public bool has(string name) {
        for (int i = 0; i < this.names.length; i++) {
            if (this.names[i] == name) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}

class result {
    private row[] rows;
    private int idx;

    construct {
        this.rows = [];
        this.idx = 0;
    }

    // Row iteration over the rows array. hasNext()/next() give the
    // rows an iteration surface so a future protocol can fill them without
    // an API change; on this build rows stay empty (no session, no
    // result-set framing -- see the honesty note), so hasNext() is always
    // false. The `.length` PROPERTY form: the .length() CALL form reads
    // garbage on the self-host for class-element arrays (the ARRAY-LENGTH
    // landmine std/sql.sr probed).
    public bool hasNext() {
        return this.idx < this.rows.length;
    }

    // The row at the cursor, advancing it; null once exhausted
    // (and always on this build).
    public row next() {
        if (this.idx >= this.rows.length) { return null; }
        row r = this.rows[this.idx];
        this.idx++;
        return r;
    }
}

class connection {
    private string host;
    private string user;
    private string pass;
    private string db;
    private string lastReply;
    private int protocolVer;
    private string serverVer;

    construct(string host, string user, string pass, string db) {
        this.host = host;
        this.user = user;
        this.pass = pass;
        this.db = db;
        this.lastReply = "";
        this.protocolVer = 0;
        this.serverVer = "";
    }

    // The greeting summary (protocol byte + server version chars) after a
    // successful greeting exchange; "" before the first connect, after a
    // failed connect, or when the peer sent no parseable greeting (packet
    // framing gives junk no text surface).
    public string getLastReply() {
        return this.lastReply;
    }

    // True only after a COMPLETED authenticated session. Always
    // false on this build -- authentication is unimplemented (see the
    // honesty note); the getter exists for the std/pop.sr/std/ssh.sr
    // failure-reporting shape.
    public bool isConnected() {
        return false;
    }

    // The greeting's server version string (e.g. "8.0.32"), ""
    // when no protocol-10 greeting is accepted.
    public string serverVersion() {
        return this.serverVer;
    }

    // The greeting's protocol version byte (10 on real MySQL
    // servers); 0 when none is accepted. This is the "did a real server
    // greet me" test callers want.
    public int protocolVersion() {
        return this.protocolVer;
    }

    // Returns an EMPTY result (no session exists to run the statement on
    // -- see the honesty note). NOTE (overload dispatch bug):
    // call sites dispatch by NAME to the last-declared overload of a name
    // regardless of arity; both overloads call the same private runQuery,
    // so either landing is behaviorally identical.
    public result query(string sql) {
        return this.runQuery(sql);
    }

    // Two-arg query; params are carried unused (no client-side
    // substitution). Deliberately does NOT call
    // this.query(sql) -- that delegation re-dispatches to the last-declared
    // same-name overload (itself) and recurses forever on both compilers
    // (see the dispatch bug note).
    public result query(string sql, object[] params) {
        return this.runQuery(sql);
    }

    // Returns 0 -- the affected-rows count is not available over this wire
    // protocol, and there is no session to run the statement on. Single
    // declaration, no overloads: unaffected by the dispatch bug.
    public int insert(string sql, object[] params) {
        return 0;
    }

    // No-op: there is no session socket
    // to close -- connect() closes its own socket before returning (no
    // authentication happens, so nothing stays open).
    public void close() { }

    // Shared implementation of both query overloads (differently-named on
    // purpose -- the dispatch bug note above). Builds the empty result the
    // honesty note describes; sql/params never reach any network.
    private result runQuery(string sql) {
        result r = new result();
        return r;
    }

    // Records what the greeting exchange observed. `info` is the NUL-free
    // summary (protocol byte + server version chars, see the NUL LANDMINE)
    // built by readGreeting(); anything not shaped like a protocol-10
    // greeting leaves lastReply empty and the getters at their construct()
    // defaults, which is the graceful-failure surface for junk/silent
    // peers. Private, and called ONLY from this module's free connect()
    // through the instance (`c.absorb(...)`) -- probed working on both
    // compilers (build/probe p2: a private method called on an instance
    // from a same-module free function compiles and runs on both;
    // unqualified calls are the shape that breaks on rs).
    private void absorb(string info) {
        if (greetingOk(info)) {
            this.protocolVer = greetingProtocol(info);
            this.serverVer = greetingVersion(info);
            this.lastReply = info;
        } else {
            this.lastReply = "";
        }
    }
}

// Wraps the four strings in a connection and then performs the one MySQL
// wire exchange that precedes any authentication -- net.connectRaw to the
// dotted-IP host on defaultPort(), the 30 s SO_RCVTIMEO armed right after
// (net.setRecvTimeoutRaw), a bounded read-and-parse of the server's
// handshake greeting (readGreeting), net.closeRaw. No HandshakeResponse41
// is ever sent and no session exists (see the honesty note); isConnected()
// stays false. On any failure (unreachable host, no greeting, non-MySQL
// peer) the connection comes back with protocolVersion() 0 and
// getLastReply() "" and the process exits cleanly.
public connection connect(string host, string user, string pass, string db) {
    connection c = new connection(host, user, pass, db);
    int sock = net.connectRaw(host, defaultPort());
    if (sock < 0) { return c; }
    net.setRecvTimeoutRaw(sock, 30000);
    string info = readGreeting(sock);
    net.closeRaw(sock);
    c.absorb(info);
    return c;
}

// Reads and parses the server's initial handshake greeting packet (the one
// MySQL wire message that precedes any authentication). Returns a NUL-free
// summary string whose first character is the greeting's protocol version
// byte (10 on every real server) followed by the server version string
// (e.g. char(10) + "8.0.32"); "" when the peer said nothing, hung up, or
// the bytes are not a protocol-10 greeting.
//
// WIRE SHAPE: the greeting packet is [3-byte payload length LE][sequence
// id][payload], payload = [protocol version byte][server version string]
// [NUL][auth-plugin-data part 1 (8 bytes)][filler][capability flags]... --
// it contains NUL bytes (header high bytes, sequence id, the version
// terminator), so the parse works on packed int[] buffers where NUL values
// survive, and returns char(proto) + the version chars.
//
// INT[] PACKING (the binary-safe path the NUL LANDMINE prescribes): the
// bytes are observed through net.recvRaw, which delivers each
// chunk as a string -- and strings are NUL-terminated char* end-to-end
// (genStringConcat CONTRACT), so the first embedded NUL is where the chunk
// text ends and the parse cannot trust byte offsets across it. The chunk
// strings are therefore accumulated and RE-PACKED one byte per int[] slot
// via string.toBytes() (the blessed binary-safe representation: NUL values
// survive in an int[] slot). The
// visible stream starts at the payload-length header byte 0 (wire[0]); the
// header NULs (length bytes 1-2, sequence id) and every payload NUL (the
// version terminator, the filler) are invisible, so the parse is anchored
// RELATIVE to the first byte:
//   wire[0] = payload length low byte (any value; NOT interpreted),
//   wire[1] = protocol version byte -- for every real greeting the header
//             NULs drop, leaving the proto byte here (probed: a 21,10,56,...
//             wire decodes proto == 10 at wire[1]),
//   wire[2..] = the version string. The version ends where the auth-plugin
//             data begins; its terminator NUL is dropped, so the terminator
//             is recovered from the wire layout: payload = proto(1) +
//             version(v) + NUL(1) + auth(8) + filler(1) + caps(...), so the
//             auth block starts 10 bytes before the LAST non-NUL payload
//             byte's wire index when caps are counted... which the NUL-free
//             view cannot pin. The parser therefore takes the version as
//             the longest run of version characters ([0-9.] and '-') from
//             wire[2] -- every real server version is such a run
//             ("8.0.32", "5.7.40", "8.0.30-0ubuntu0.20.04.1") and the
//             scramble/auth bytes that follow are arbitrary; when the run
//             would absorb printable auth bytes (a ~4%% chance for random
//             auth data) the version tail reads long -- the same lenient
//             bannerOk posture accepted for shapes that carried
//             byte 10 at the proto position and a NUL afterwards.
//
// The returned summary is char(wire[1]) + the version characters -- the
// NUL-free summary feeding the greetingOk/greetingProtocol/greetingVersion
// helpers.
//
// BOUNDED: at most 16 net.recvRaw calls of up to 1024 bytes (~16 KB) and
// one 30 s SO_RCVTIMEO per call (armed by connect) -- no path can spin or
// hang. A read returning "" (disconnect, timeout, or the zero-extended
// SOCKET_ERROR net.recvRaw reports as "") ends accumulation; the parse
// then either already succeeded or returns "".
private string readGreeting(int sock) {
    string acc = "";
    for (int guard = 0; guard < 16; guard++) {
        string chunk = net.recvRaw(sock, 1024);
        if (chunk.length <= 0) { break; }
        acc += chunk;
        if (acc.length >= 3) { break; }
    }
    if (acc.length < 3) { return ""; }
    int[] wire = acc.toBytes();
    int proto = wire[1];
    if (proto != 10) { return ""; }
    string info = "";
    info += char(proto);
    for (int i = 2; i < wire.length; i++) {
        int b = wire[i];
        bool verChar = (b >= 48 && b <= 57) || b == 46 || b == 45;
        if (!verChar) { break; }
        info += char(b);
    }
    if (info.length < 2) { return ""; }
    return info;
}

// Copies s[start .. end) character by character. Deliberately NOT substr:
// rs's two-arg substr treats X as an END offset while the self-host treats
// it as a LENGTH -- identical only when start is 0 (probed; see
// silverc-rs/src/string_ops.rs emit_string_substring) -- and every caller
// here slices from a nonzero offset.
private string sliceRange(string s, int start, int end) {
    string result = "";
    for (int i = start; i < end; i++) {
        result += s[i];
    }
    return result;
}
